1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,999
A status page says "3 commits
ahead". Ahead of what?

2
00:00:08,339 --> 00:00:13,157
Here's a second puzzle. An engineer opens
a review for a small change, one commit,

3
00:00:13,197 --> 00:00:16,653
on a branch they created from
a colleague's feature branch.

4
00:00:16,693 --> 00:00:21,570
The review shows forty changed files. Their
one commit is in there somewhere, under

5
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:25,776
everything the colleague did. What
went wrong? Say your guess out loud.

6
00:00:30,616 --> 00:00:35,815
Nothing is broken. The engineer assumed that
Git knows which branch their branch was

7
00:00:35,855 --> 00:00:39,432
created from, and compares
against that. Git doesn't know.

8
00:00:39,472 --> 00:00:43,860
Nothing in a repository records which
branch a branch was created from.

9
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:48,912
Every comparison needs a base that you
name, and "3 commits ahead" is meaningless

10
00:00:48,952 --> 00:00:52,529
until you say ahead of what.
Hold on to those forty files.

11
00:00:52,569 --> 00:00:53,465
They come back.

12
00:00:55,305 --> 00:00:59,049
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:59,089 --> 00:01:03,081
A commit is one saved snapshot,
and it points back at its parent.

14
00:01:03,121 --> 00:01:07,672
A branch is a name that points at one
commit. You know what one branch is.

15
00:01:07,712 --> 00:01:10,463
This video is about the
relation between two.

16
00:01:10,803 --> 00:01:15,610
First, the arithmetic: divergence, the
merge base, and how to count how far each

17
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:20,456
side is ahead. Then the test behind "merged"
and "can be fast-forwarded": is one

18
00:01:20,496 --> 00:01:25,363
commit an ancestor of another? Then the
absence that surprises people: Git has no

19
00:01:25,403 --> 00:01:29,847
parent-branch concept. And last, a
preview of remote-tracking branches and

20
00:01:29,887 --> 00:01:34,754
upstreams. "Ahead" and "behind" in git
status are this same arithmetic, against a

21
00:01:34,794 --> 00:01:37,480
ref that is only as fresh
as your last fetch.

22
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:40,898
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:41,238 --> 00:01:42,650
After this video you can:

24
00:01:42,990 --> 00:01:47,120
Compute the merge base of two branches,
and count how far each is ahead.

25
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:49,941
Test whether one commit is
an ancestor of another.

26
00:01:50,281 --> 00:01:54,153
Explain why Git cannot say which
branch a branch was created from.

27
00:01:54,493 --> 00:01:58,937
Read ahead and behind against a remote-tracking
branch as a statement about the last

28
00:01:58,977 --> 00:01:59,257
fetch.

29
00:02:01,097 --> 00:02:05,646
Divergence and ancestry. In one sentence:
two branches have diverged when neither

30
00:02:05,686 --> 00:02:10,293
tip is an ancestor of the other. Their
merge base is the best common ancestor, and

31
00:02:10,333 --> 00:02:14,882
"ahead" and "behind" are counted from it.
A tip is the commit a branch points at.

32
00:02:14,922 --> 00:02:18,735
An ancestor is any commit you reach
by walking back through parents.

33
00:02:19,075 --> 00:02:22,887
Precisely. A common ancestor is a
commit reachable from both tips.

34
00:02:22,927 --> 00:02:27,731
The manual says: "One common ancestor is
better than another common ancestor if the

35
00:02:27,771 --> 00:02:31,758
latter is an ancestor of the former",
and a best one is a merge base.

36
00:02:31,798 --> 00:02:35,376
There can be more than one, which
the videos on merge take up.

37
00:02:35,716 --> 00:02:41,641
Ranges are sets of commits. A..B, two dots,
is everything reachable from B but not

38
00:02:41,681 --> 00:02:47,679
from A. A...B, three dots, is the symmetric
difference: reachable from one side but

39
00:02:47,719 --> 00:02:53,717
not both. git merge-base --is-ancestor A B
exits with 0 when A is an ancestor of B,

40
00:02:53,757 --> 00:02:55,608
and with 1 when it is not.

41
00:02:55,948 --> 00:03:00,685
The dots mean something else in git diff.
This is a known trap, and the textbook

42
00:03:00,725 --> 00:03:03,850
gives it a table. Almost
everyone falls into it once.

43
00:03:04,190 --> 00:03:09,750
In git log and git rev-list, A..B is the
commits reachable from B and not from A,

44
00:03:09,790 --> 00:03:13,690
and A...B is the commits
reachable from exactly one side.

45
00:03:13,730 --> 00:03:18,874
In git diff, A..B compares the two tips
directly, the same as git diff A B.

46
00:03:18,914 --> 00:03:23,782
And A...B compares from the merge
base of A and B to B: what B changed.

47
00:03:24,122 --> 00:03:29,089
No parent branch. In one sentence: nothing
in a repository records which branch a

48
00:03:29,129 --> 00:03:33,910
branch was created from. The only trace
is a line in a local reflog, and every

49
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:38,916
comparison needs a base that you name.
Look at the picture: three branch names on

50
00:03:38,956 --> 00:03:42,625
one line of commits, and nothing
says which came from which.

51
00:03:42,965 --> 00:03:46,301
Look for the relationship
in each place it could be.

52
00:03:46,341 --> 00:03:50,911
The new ref holds an ID. The commit
it names has no field for a branch.

53
00:03:50,951 --> 00:03:56,106
The local configuration has no entry for
it. The only mention is the reflog line

54
00:03:56,146 --> 00:04:01,560
branch: Created from the other branch. The
reflog is a local journal of where a name

55
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:06,884
has pointed. So that line exists in this
clone and nowhere else, and git branch -D

56
00:04:06,924 --> 00:04:08,703
or reflog expiry removes it.

57
00:04:09,043 --> 00:04:13,310
git branch --contains answers
"which branches reach this commit".

58
00:04:13,350 --> 00:04:15,430
That's reachability, not origin.

59
00:04:15,770 --> 00:04:20,716
The nearest thing to an answer is a guess.
The field %(is-base:<commit>) of git

60
00:04:20,756 --> 00:04:26,016
for-each-ref marks, in the manual's words,
"the ref that is most likely the ref used

61
00:04:26,056 --> 00:04:29,866
as a starting point for the
branch that produced" the commit.

62
00:04:29,906 --> 00:04:34,977
It's chosen by a first-parent heuristic,
and the guess changes with the refs that

63
00:04:35,017 --> 00:04:40,089
exist. It exists since Git 2.47, and the
textbook's recommendation is: a hint for

64
00:04:40,129 --> 00:04:42,423
humans, never an input to automation.

65
00:04:42,763 --> 00:04:47,716
This is why git rebase and git merge take
the target as an argument: Git can't infer

66
00:04:47,756 --> 00:04:47,895
it.

67
00:04:53,235 --> 00:04:58,003
Remote-tracking branches and upstream. In
one sentence: a remote-tracking branch

68
00:04:58,043 --> 00:05:02,931
such as origin/main is a ref in your own
repository that records the last position

69
00:05:02,971 --> 00:05:07,860
of a branch in another repository, and an
upstream is two configuration lines that

70
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:09,603
pair a local branch with one.

71
00:05:09,943 --> 00:05:13,423
Remote-tracking branches live
under refs/remotes/<remote>/.

72
00:05:13,463 --> 00:05:18,436
When a teammate pushes, your origin/main
stays where it was, until git fetch updates

73
00:05:18,476 --> 00:05:18,615
it.

74
00:05:18,955 --> 00:05:24,540
The upstream of a branch B is configured
by branch.B.remote and branch.B.merge, and

75
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:29,284
B@{upstream}, or @{u}, names the
corresponding remote-tracking branch.

76
00:05:29,324 --> 00:05:33,892
git status -sb and git branch -v
compare a branch with its upstream.

77
00:05:33,932 --> 00:05:37,077
Nothing in that comparison
talks to the server.

78
00:05:37,417 --> 00:05:41,115
A branch created from another
local branch has no upstream.

79
00:05:41,155 --> 00:05:45,738
A branch created from a remote-tracking
branch gets one automatically, by

80
00:05:45,778 --> 00:05:48,652
branch.autoSetupMerge,
which defaults to true.

81
00:05:48,992 --> 00:05:54,907
Risk labels. Everything in this video is 🟢
SAFE, reading state or adding a ref, with

82
00:05:54,947 --> 00:06:00,295
two exceptions that appear once each.
git branch -D, 🔴 DANGEROUS, whose five

83
00:06:00,335 --> 00:06:04,549
questions video 23 answered.
And git fetch, which is 🟢 SAFE.

84
00:06:04,889 --> 00:06:09,711
When this arithmetic is the wrong tool.
When the question is about content and not

85
00:06:09,751 --> 00:06:14,216
commits. A squash merge puts a branch's
content into main without any of its

86
00:06:14,256 --> 00:06:19,078
commits, and then the ranges say "three
commits ahead" about work that has landed.

87
00:06:19,118 --> 00:06:21,568
The next video deals with that blind spot.

88
00:06:23,408 --> 00:06:28,719
Picture a road that forks. Two cars start
at the fork and drive down different arms.

89
00:06:28,759 --> 00:06:34,007
The fork is the merge base. "Ahead by three"
is how far one car has driven from the

90
00:06:34,047 --> 00:06:37,256
fork, and "behind by two"
is how far the other has.

91
00:06:37,596 --> 00:06:41,175
Now the limit of the picture,
which is the point of the video.

92
00:06:41,215 --> 00:06:46,078
On a real road, each arm has a name painted
on a sign at the fork, so you know which

93
00:06:46,118 --> 00:06:50,981
arm branched off which. In Git there is no
sign. The fork is a commit, and both arms

94
00:06:51,021 --> 00:06:55,650
are equally "the road". Which one you call
the trunk is your decision, made each

95
00:06:55,690 --> 00:06:56,642
time you compare.

96
00:06:56,982 --> 00:07:01,270
And for remote-tracking branches: origin/main
is a photograph of where the other car

97
00:07:01,310 --> 00:07:05,339
was when you last looked. Until you look
again, with a fetch, every "ahead" and

98
00:07:05,379 --> 00:07:07,606
"behind" is measured
against the photograph.

99
00:07:09,446 --> 00:07:13,799
Now the textbook's drawing, with
the counts. Two commits are shared.

100
00:07:13,839 --> 00:07:19,161
The fork is at 03f74b9: that's the merge
base. main has two commits of its own, and

101
00:07:19,201 --> 00:07:24,523
feature/rouge has three. Three numbers and
a merge base describe the relation: two,

102
00:07:24,563 --> 00:07:25,751
three, and 03f74b9.

103
00:07:30,091 --> 00:07:34,391
Into the lab. main and feature/rouge
after both received commits.

104
00:07:34,431 --> 00:07:39,799
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. From
the graph alone, write the merge base.

105
00:07:39,839 --> 00:07:44,406
Then write how many commits
main..feature/rouge selects, and how many

106
00:07:44,446 --> 00:07:47,477
feature/rouge..main. Say
your answer out loud.

107
00:07:52,317 --> 00:07:57,940
Check your paper. The merge base is 03f74b9.
main..feature/rouge is what the feature

108
00:07:57,980 --> 00:08:03,063
has that main lacks: three commits.
feature/rouge..main is the reverse: two.

109
00:08:03,403 --> 00:08:08,573
--left-right --count prints the two
numbers in the order of the operands: two

110
00:08:08,613 --> 00:08:12,295
commits only on the left side,
three only on the right.

111
00:08:12,335 --> 00:08:17,911
The %(ahead-behind:main) field of git
for-each-ref computes the same pair for every

112
00:08:17,951 --> 00:08:21,159
branch at once, from the
branch's point of view.

113
00:08:21,499 --> 00:08:25,477
Now the yes-or-no test. Predict
the three exit statuses.

114
00:08:25,517 --> 00:08:27,558
Say them out loud. I'll wait.

115
00:08:32,398 --> 00:08:36,024
main is not an ancestor of
the feature branch: status 1.

116
00:08:36,064 --> 00:08:39,756
So merging the feature into
main can't be a fast-forward.

117
00:08:39,796 --> 00:08:43,945
main~2, the merge base, is an
ancestor of both: status 0, twice.

118
00:08:44,285 --> 00:08:49,657
Quick quiz. In git diff, which form
lists only the feature's own work?

119
00:08:49,697 --> 00:08:52,749
A, two dots. B, three dots. Your answer?

120
00:08:57,589 --> 00:09:03,094
B, three dots. That's the dots trap. git
diff --stat main...feature/rouge, three

121
00:09:03,134 --> 00:09:08,917
dots, lists the feature's own work.
main..feature/rouge, two dots, also shows main's

122
00:09:08,957 --> 00:09:10,442
two commits, reversed.

123
00:09:10,782 --> 00:09:14,720
Create a branch from feature/rouge,
and look for the relationship.

124
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:18,516
Predict where, if anywhere, the
name feature/rouge is recorded.

125
00:09:18,556 --> 00:09:19,782
Make your prediction.

126
00:09:24,622 --> 00:09:28,546
The new ref holds an ID. The
commit has no field for a branch.

127
00:09:28,586 --> 00:09:33,852
The configuration has no entry. The only
mention is the reflog line branch: Created

128
00:09:33,892 --> 00:09:35,067
from feature/rouge.

129
00:09:35,407 --> 00:09:39,781
git branch --contains feature/rouge
lists both branches: the commits of

130
00:09:39,821 --> 00:09:44,257
feature/rouge are on feature/rouge-stemming
as much as on feature/rouge.

131
00:09:44,597 --> 00:09:49,399
You choose the base. Against main, three
commits. Against feature/rouge, one.

132
00:09:49,439 --> 00:09:53,738
Then the script deletes feature/rouge
with git branch -D 🔴 DANGEROUS.

133
00:09:53,778 --> 00:09:58,895
Its commits remain reachable from the
stemming branch, so no commit loses its last

134
00:09:58,935 --> 00:10:04,052
name here. And the first answer doesn't
change, because the commits never belonged

135
00:10:04,092 --> 00:10:04,806
to a branch.

136
00:10:07,646 --> 00:10:12,839
%(is-base:...): the guess, and how
it changes with the refs that exist.

137
00:10:12,879 --> 00:10:18,955
First it marks feature/rouge. Delete that
branch, and the same question marks main.

138
00:10:21,795 --> 00:10:27,670
Two refs, same ID, two namespaces:
refs/heads/main and refs/remotes/origin/main.

139
00:10:28,010 --> 00:10:31,464
@{upstream} resolves through the
two configuration entries to

140
00:10:31,504 --> 00:10:32,896
refs/remotes/origin/main.

141
00:10:33,236 --> 00:10:37,184
Now Asha pushes a commit to
the server from her own clone.

142
00:10:37,224 --> 00:10:41,790
Your repository hasn't been told.
Predict what git status -sb says.

143
00:10:41,830 --> 00:10:42,890
Say it out loud.

144
00:10:47,730 --> 00:10:52,696
Before the fetch, no difference, because
origin/main is what your repository last

145
00:10:52,736 --> 00:10:57,516
heard. git fetch 🟢 SAFE moves origin/main,
and the status changes to behind 1.

146
00:10:57,556 --> 00:11:02,707
So "Up to date with origin/main" is a
statement about your copy of the remote, never

147
00:11:02,747 --> 00:11:04,190
about the remote itself.

148
00:11:04,530 --> 00:11:07,975
A branch created from another
local branch has no upstream.

149
00:11:08,015 --> 00:11:12,936
A branch created from a remote-tracking
branch gets one automatically, which is what

150
00:11:12,976 --> 00:11:14,885
the line set up to track reports.

151
00:11:16,725 --> 00:11:18,373
Five mistakes to watch for.

152
00:11:18,713 --> 00:11:23,500
Saying "3 commits ahead" without a base.
Root cause: ahead and behind are counted

153
00:11:23,540 --> 00:11:28,209
from a merge base with a second ref, and
Git has no default "parent" to compare

154
00:11:28,249 --> 00:11:28,507
with.

155
00:11:28,847 --> 00:11:32,745
Using two dots in git diff to
see what a branch changed.

156
00:11:32,785 --> 00:11:38,442
Root cause: in git diff, A..B compares the
tips directly; A...B compares from the

157
00:11:38,482 --> 00:11:39,215
merge base.

158
00:11:39,555 --> 00:11:45,118
Asking Git which branch a branch was created
from. Root cause: a branch stores one

159
00:11:45,158 --> 00:11:49,423
commit ID and no origin; the only
trace is a local reflog line.

160
00:11:49,763 --> 00:11:54,103
Reading "up to date with 'origin/main'"
as the state of the server.

161
00:11:54,143 --> 00:11:59,267
Root cause: origin/main is the last-known
state, updated only by fetch or push.

162
00:11:59,607 --> 00:12:05,441
Feeding %(is-base:...) to automation. Root
cause: it is a first-parent heuristic

163
00:12:05,481 --> 00:12:08,818
whose answer changes with
the refs that exist.

164
00:12:10,658 --> 00:12:15,457
Now, out of the lab. Stacked branches
are where this bites, as the textbook

165
00:12:15,497 --> 00:12:20,361
describes. Branch B was created from
branch A. A is merged with a squash, or

166
00:12:20,401 --> 00:12:25,653
deleted. B's "changes against main" suddenly
include everything A did, because the

167
00:12:25,693 --> 00:12:30,686
base you implied no longer exists. That's
the forty-file review from the hook.

168
00:12:30,726 --> 00:12:34,815
The repair is git rebase --onto,
which moves B to its real base.

169
00:12:34,855 --> 00:12:36,558
The rebase videos cover it.

170
00:12:36,898 --> 00:12:42,162
And the general rule for reports and
dashboards. [ahead 2, behind 3] in git status,

171
00:12:42,202 --> 00:12:47,467
the counts on a pull request, "can this be
fast-forwarded", and "which commits does

172
00:12:47,507 --> 00:12:52,644
this release contain" are all this arithmetic.
Compute them with ranges, not with

173
00:12:52,684 --> 00:12:54,306
dates, and state the base.

174
00:12:56,146 --> 00:13:00,332
